# Stack conventions

Stack (see `AGENTS.md` section 5): **Next.js + TypeScript**, **Supabase** (Postgres, Auth, RLS, Storage), **Gemini Flash** (initial), **Upstash Redis** (tentative), **Vercel**.
Do not add or swap layers without approval. Verify package names and APIs against the installed versions and official docs; do not rely on memory for version-specific details.

## Supabase clients (keep these separate)
1. **Browser client**: anon key, used only in client components for things that are safe under RLS.
2. **Server client** (per request, cookie-aware, user-scoped): default for server code. Runs *as the user*, so RLS applies.
3. **Admin client** (service role): bypasses RLS. Only in server-only modules (mark with the `server-only` package if approved), only for admin-justified operations, never imported by client code.
Never create these clients ad hoc inside components. Keep one factory per type in a clear folder (e.g. `lib/supabase/`).

## Auth
- Use Supabase Auth for email/password and OAuth. Do not hand-roll sessions or password handling.
- Keep the session fresh on the server using the officially recommended Next.js integration for the installed Supabase version (check docs; it has changed over time).
- Protect routes on the **server** (middleware/route handlers/server actions + RLS). Client-side redirects are only UX.
- Store the role (`user` / `admin`) in a place the user cannot edit (not in user-editable metadata). Use it in RLS policies and server checks.

## Database workflow
- All schema changes go through **migration files** (Supabase CLI, `supabase/migrations/`), never by clicking in the dashboard. Schema changes need user approval first.
- RLS enabled on every table in an exposed schema, default deny, explicit policies, each documented in the migration.
- Generate TypeScript types from the database schema (Supabase CLI) and use them instead of hand-written row types. Regenerate after every migration.
- Seed data for development and tests goes in a seed file; never commit real user data.
- Test RLS with at least two users plus an admin (see `30-testing.mdc`).

## Rate limiting (Upstash Redis, tentative)
- Use it for per-user/IP limits on: auth endpoints, flag creation, LLM-backed endpoints.
- Return `429` with the standard error shape and a retry hint.
- Wrap the limiter in a small interface so it can be mocked in tests and replaced later.
- Credentials are server-side env vars only.

## LLM (Gemini)
- Follow `70-llm-integration.mdc`. Model name from env/config, not hardcoded. Server-side only.

## Vercel
- Env vars are managed per environment (Development / Preview / Production). Never commit them. Keep `.env.example` in sync with the variable **names**.
- Serverless function time and size limits apply; design LLM and fetch work accordingly (timeouts, retries, caching).
- Do not rely on local filesystem writes or in-memory state persisting between requests.
- Production deploys happen only when the user asks. Do not run deploy commands on your own.

## Local development
- Prefer the Supabase local stack (CLI) for development if the user sets it up; otherwise use a separate **development** Supabase project, never the production one.
- Never run destructive database commands (reset, drop) against a hosted project without explicit approval.
